<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <GenerateAssemblyInfo>false</GenerateAssemblyInfo>

    <!-- 1. Configure NuGet Package generation and XML Documentation -->
    <GeneratePackageOnBuild>true</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
        
    <!-- 2. NuGet Package Metadata Info -->
    <PackageId>ArrangeAlgorithms</PackageId>
    <Version>3.0.0</Version>
    <Authors>Nguyen Thang</Authors>
    <Company>Nguyen Thang</Company>
    <Description>A 2D label placement library for engineering drawings that automatically arranges labels to prevent overlaps with each other and blocked regions. Features 5 layout algorithms: Greedy, Bounded Backtracking, Simulated Annealing, Force-Directed, and Constraint Satisfaction. The geometry it works in comes from the PlaneGeometry package, which shipped inside this one up to version 2.0.0.</Description>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/nguyenthanguth/ArrangeAlgorithms</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/nguyenthanguth/ArrangeAlgorithms</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <Copyright>Copyright © 2026 Nguyen Thang</Copyright>
    <PackageTags>label-placement geometry cad autocad tekla algorithm greedy backtracking simulated-annealing force-directed csp</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>
      BREAKING CHANGES — the geometry left this package

      Version 2.0.0 was one package holding both the placement algorithms and the 2D geometry they run
      on. The geometry was most of the code and depended on none of the placement work, so it is now
      the PlaneGeometry package, and the types both it and SolidGeometry need are in CommonGeometry.
      Both come with this package as dependencies; what changes is the names you import.

      NAMESPACES
      - ArrangeAlgorithms (for Tolerance) becomes CommonGeometry.
      - ArrangeAlgorithms.Datatype becomes CommonGeometry.Datatype.
      - ArrangeAlgorithms.Enums becomes CommonGeometry.Enums.
      - ArrangeAlgorithms.Core becomes PlaneGeometry.Core.
      - ArrangeAlgorithms.Geometry becomes PlaneGeometry.Geometry.
      - ArrangeAlgorithms and ArrangeAlgorithms.Algorithms are unchanged, so Arrange, ArrangeOptions,
        ArrangeAlgorithmType, IArrangeAlgorithm and the five algorithms keep their names and their API.

      TYPE NAMES
      - Every 2D type gained a 2, matching the 3 SolidGeometry already used: GeoPoint2, GeoVector2,
        GeoLine2, GeoPolyline2, GeoPolygon2, GeoCircle2, GeoRectangle2, and Collision2, Containment2,
        Distance2, Intersection2, Merge2, Parallel2, Parametrization2, Projection2, Splition2.
      - Parallel2 no longer collides with System.Threading.Tasks.Parallel, which the old name did.
      - [TypeForwardedTo] cannot soften any of this. It only works when a type keeps its full name,
        and these changed namespace, so the using directives have to be updated by hand.

      TWO THINGS THAT BITE QUIETLY
      - Tolerance.Global is now one setting shared with SolidGeometry rather than one each. Setting it
        for a drawing in the plane sets it for a model in space too.
      - Code sitting in a namespace under ArrangeAlgorithms that also imports a library with its own
        Tolerance - Autodesk.AutoCAD.Geometry does - will now see CS0104, because ours arrives through
        a using rather than through the enclosing namespace, so the two tie where they did not before.
        Name the one you mean: using Tolerance = CommonGeometry.Tolerance;
      - Tolerance gained a fourth threshold, EqualPlanar, which only SolidGeometry reads. It shows up
        in Tolerance.ToString, and the three-argument constructor sets it to equalPoint.

      REMOVED
      - EnumerableExtension. Nothing in the repository called it, and its MaxBy/MinBy collide with
        System.Linq on .NET 6 and later.
    </PackageReleaseNotes>

    <!-- 3. Embed source code and Debug symbols into DLL to support F12 original code viewing -->
    <DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
    <EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
    <GenerateDependencyFile>false</GenerateDependencyFile>

  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PlaneGeometry\PlaneGeometry.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>